Aim
To simulate a single server single queueing system using GPSS and study customer waiting time, service time, and server utilization.

Theory
A Single Server Single Queueing System is one of the most basic models used in simulation. 
In this system, customers arrive one by one and wait in a single queue until the server becomes free. Only one customer can receive service at a time. 
This type of system is commonly used in banks, barber shops, ticket counters, hospital reception desks, and billing counters.
In GPSS simulation, transactions represent customers. The GENERATE block is used to create customers at specific time intervals. 
The QUEUE block stores the arriving customers in a waiting line. The SEIZE block allows a customer to occupy the server or facility. 
If the server is busy, the customer waits in the queue. After getting service, the customer leaves the system using the TERMINATE block.
The ADVANCE block represents the service time taken by the server. The RELEASE block frees the server after the service is completed so that the next customer can be served.
This simulation helps in understanding:

Waiting time of customers
Server utilization
Queue length
Average service time
System performance

Conclusion
Thus, the simulation of a single server single queueing system was successfully performed using GPSS. 
The experiment helped in understanding how customers wait in a queue and receive service from a single server. 
The output report showed the server utilization, average waiting time, and queue statistics.
